Here are the CRUD endpoints with Zod validation:

```javascript
// routes/tasks.js
router.get('/tasks', taskController.getAll);
router.get('/tasks/:id', taskController.getById);
router.post('/tasks', validate(createTaskSchema), taskController.create);
router.put('/tasks/:id', validate(updateTaskSchema), taskController.update);
router.delete('/tasks/:id', taskController.delete);
```

Each controller method includes try-catch blocks with appropriate HTTP status codes (200, 201, 400, 404, 500).

I'd use Zustand slices pattern to keep things modular:

```typescript
// stores/dashboardStore.ts
interface DashboardSlice {
  filters: { dateRange: [Date, Date]; status: string[]; priority: string[] };
  setFilter: (key: string, value: any) => void;
  resetFilters: () => void;
}

interface RealtimeSlice {
  metrics: MetricData[];
  notifications: Notification[];
  wsStatus: 'connected' | 'disconnected';
  addMetric: (metric: MetricData) => void;
}

export const useDashboardStore = create<DashboardSlice & RealtimeSlice>()(
  devtools(
    persist(
      (set) => ({ ...createDashboardSlice(set), ...createRealtimeSlice(set) }),
      { name: 'dashboard-storage', partialize: (state) => ({ filters: state.filters }) }
    )
  )
);
```

Key decisions: only persist filters (not real-time data), use devtools for debugging.
